Z||000000||Carrolles||02-07-2000||01:04 PM||||I just installed the high output headlights that I got from Walmart.  They are the Sylvania Halogen (I forget the suffix) that come boxed in a set of two for around $16.00.  Boy are they nice.<P>I always thought the headlights on my 85SE left something to be desired.  These new bulbs are great.<P>I had to pull out the "Do-It Yourself" manual to figure how to get the two bottom screws out.  I couldn't find anything in the Helms manual.<P>This is about the most involved procedure for changing headlamps I've ever encountered.<P>Anyway the new bulbs are very much worth the trouble.||||reg||14
Z||000001||theogre||02-07-2000||02:46 PM||||silly,<P>I posted an easier way to get the bulbs out awhile back. I'm going to have to start collecting some of my old stuff up and put into tunnel 6. We're all starting cover the same things over and over.<P>I was wondering how the sylvanias were. I have the Phillips High Vis and like them allot....||||reg||1
Z||000002||WGraffiKs||02-07-2000||03:53 PM||||I've never had a problem with changing the sealed-beams.  First, I turn on the lights, then with my thumb, I pull down (out) the top of the light switch.  This leaves the lights in the "up" position, but the lamps "off" I can't remember if it was necessary to remove the black plastic trim piece (two torx screws, left and right side of the lamp), but that should be easy if it's necessary. Then, I simply remove the top two bezel screws and bend the bezel down.  Slip the old beam up and out, slip the new down and in, and tighten the two screws.   For the bezel, the key is to press the screwdriver in HARD when first turning the screw, to prevent the screwhead from stripping.  I may have left out a few small tidbits, since it's been a while since I've had to change one.  I don't ever expect to have to change the HID lamps on my 86.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-07-2000||07:17 PM||||good way to break the frames doing that. I wouldn't do that more than a couple times. Easer and safer to just yank out the linkage bolt and flip the whole lift over backwards. Then all the screws are easily accessable with plane shaft torx drivers.||||reg||1
